 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to browsers via self-hosted WebSockets. Subsecond updates are the target; 15-second freshness under normal connectivity remains the hard requirement.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Self-hosted WebSockets were chosen over five-second HTTPS polling and hosted realtime services. Polling was rejected because its cursor-query model adds repeated read load and inherent lag. Hosted realtime was rejected to avoid recurring fees, provider coupling, and credential management. Self-hosted sockets accept the operational burden of connection capacity and reconnect logic in exchange for fast updates without a vendor bill.
+
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store provides revision history and an outbox for publication replay. Delivery manages socket sessions, handles reconnect/catch-up, and publishes from the outbox. Browser sync uses the socket subscription model with explicit reconnect and catch-up logic.
 
