 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Chosen over five-second HTTPS polling and hosted realtime services. Polling was rejected for its inherent lag and repeated read load. Hosted realtime was rejected for recurring fees, provider coupling, and credential management. Self-hosted sockets accept connection capacity and reconnect operations work in exchange for fast updates without a vendor bill. This decision cascades into the change store (outbox for replay), delivery (session management, reconnect/catch-up), and browser sync (WebSocket subscription with catch-up).
